---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import StepList from "../../../components/StepList.astro";
import Step from "../../../components/Step.astro";
---

<Article
  title="How Gloomhaven Works: A Beginner's Guide to the Core Loop"
  description="Two cards a turn, no dice, and a hand that runs out. The system explained without the rulebook."
  game="gloomhaven"
  gameTitle="Gloomhaven"
  category="Rules"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Gloomhaven has a reputation for being impenetrable, and the rulebook doesn't
    help. But the underlying system is elegant and it fits in one article. This
    isn't a replacement for the rules — it's what you'd want explained before
    deciding whether to buy in.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Each turn you secretly commit two ability cards from a limited hand. One sets your speed, and you use the top of one and the bottom of the other. Your hand never refreshes fully — when it runs out, you're done for the scenario." />

  <h2>There Are No Dice</h2>

  <p>
    Combat is resolved with a personal <strong>attack modifier deck</strong>. You
    declare an attack, flip the top card, and it adjusts your damage — a little
    up, a little down, occasionally a critical or a complete miss.
  </p>

  <p>
    The clever part is that this deck is yours and it changes. As the campaign
    progresses you remove bad cards and add good ones, so your character
    physically becomes more reliable over time. It's the same tension as dice,
    but it improves with the story.
  </p>

  <h2>The Card System Is the Whole Game</h2>

  <p>
    Every character has a hand of ability cards — a small hand, usually somewhere
    between eight and a dozen depending on the class. Each card has a
    <strong>top action</strong>, a <strong>bottom action</strong>, and an
    <strong>initiative number</strong>.
  </p>

  <StepList title="What a round looks like">
    <Step number={1} title="Everyone chooses two cards, in secret">
      You pick two cards from your hand. Nobody may say exactly what they're
      playing — you can discuss plans in general terms, but not name cards or
      numbers.
    </Step>
    <Step number={2} title="Reveal and set the order">
      Cards flip face up. You nominate one of your two for its initiative number,
      and that determines when you act relative to everyone else, monsters
      included.
    </Step>
    <Step number={3} title="Act on your turn">
      Use the <strong>top action of one card</strong> and the
      <strong>bottom action of the other</strong>. Never both halves of the same
      card. This is the central puzzle: every card offers two things and you can
      only ever take one from each.
    </Step>
    <Step number={4} title="Monsters act on their initiative">
      Monsters follow a deck of behaviour cards — no AI to argue about, no
      dungeon master. They move and attack according to fixed, published rules.
    </Step>
  </StepList>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Two cards, four options, pick two halves">
    If this is the only thing you take away, it's the right thing. Choosing which
    half of which card to use is where Gloomhaven's tactical depth lives, and it's
    why a hand of eleven cards produces far more decisions than eleven sounds like.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Your Hand Runs Out — That's the Timer</h2>

  <p>
    Cards you play go to your discard pile. To get them back you take a
    <strong>rest</strong>, which returns your discards to your hand but forces you
    to permanently <strong>lose</strong> one of them for the rest of the scenario.
  </p>

  <p>
    So your hand shrinks over time no matter what you do. When you can no longer
    play two cards, you become <strong>exhausted</strong> and you're out of the
    scenario.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Exhaustion is the real enemy, not damage">
    New players focus on hit points. Experienced players count cards. You will
    lose far more scenarios to running out of cards than to being killed, and
    every "lost card" ability you use is time taken off your own clock.
  </InfoBox>

  <p>
    Some powerful abilities require you to lose the card permanently to use them.
    That trade — a big effect now against a shorter scenario — is the most
    consistently interesting decision in the game.
  </p>

  <h2>Elements</h2>

  <p>
    Certain abilities generate an element — fire, ice, air, earth, light or dark.
    A generated element sits in a "strong" state, fades to "waning" after a round,
    and then disappears.
  </p>

  <p>
    Other abilities can consume an element to become much stronger. This creates
    small, satisfying combos both within a character and across the party: one
    player generates fire, another spends it the following round. It's optional
    depth — you can ignore elements entirely and still play fine — but it's where
    parties start to feel like a team.
  </p>

  <h2>Between Scenarios</h2>

  <p>
    Gloomhaven is a campaign, and the layer around the combat is a real part of it:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>You level up</strong>, adding stronger cards to your available pool</li>
    <li><strong>You buy items</strong> from the town's shop, which grows over time</li>
    <li><strong>You improve your attack modifier deck</strong> as you progress</li>
    <li><strong>Story events</strong> in the city and on the road present choices with consequences</li>
    <li><strong>The map unlocks</strong> as scenarios branch and reveal new destinations</li>
  </ul>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Retirement is a feature, not an ending">
    Each character has a personal quest. Completing it retires that character
    permanently — and unlocks a new class for you to play. It feels wrong the
    first time and it's genuinely one of the best things in the game. Retiring is
    how the box opens up.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>What It Feels Like to Play</h2>

  <p>
    A scenario is a tight tactical puzzle with a countdown built into your own
    deck. You're managing damage, position, elements and — above all — how many
    turns you have left before your hand gives out. Parties that plan together
    win; parties that improvise run out of cards.
  </p>

  <p>
    If that sounds appealing, the next question is which box to buy — see
    <a href="/games/gloomhaven/gloomhaven-vs-jaws-of-the-lion/">Gloomhaven vs Jaws of the Lion</a>.
    If you're worried about the time commitment, that's covered in
    <a href="/games/gloomhaven/how-long-does-gloomhaven-take/">how long it actually takes</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
